## Decoding the question
A **short note of 10 marks**: about **550–650 words** under four or five headings — definition, origin and thinkers with dates, core ideas, achievements, criticisms, a Pakistani example and a balanced close. A small figure earns extra credit.

## The model answer

### 1. Meaning

#### i) Definition
"Radical" comes from the Latin ***radix***, meaning **root**. **Radical feminism** holds that **patriarchy** — the system of male power over women — is the **oldest and deepest** form of oppression, present in every society and every area of life.

#### ii) The remedy
Because patriarchy runs through the **family, sexuality, the body and culture**, legal reform is not enough. Society must be **transformed at the root**. A past MCQ put it simply: **replacing patriarchy** is a **radical** idea.

### 2. Origin and thinkers

#### i) Origin
Radical feminism arose in the USA in the **late 1960s**. Women active in the civil-rights and anti-war "**New Left**" found that male comrades who preached equality expected them to make coffee and stay silent. They formed groups such as **New York Radical Women** (==1967==) and **Redstockings** (==1969==).

#### ii) Thinkers
- **Kate Millett** (*Sexual Politics*, ==1970==): relations between the sexes are **power relations**; she made "**patriarchy**" the key concept.
- **Shulamith Firestone** (*The Dialectic of Sex*, ==1970==): oppression began in **reproduction**; women are a "**sex class**".
- **Carol Hanisch** ("The Personal Is Political", ==1970==): women's private problems are **political**.
- **Susan Brownmiller** (*Against Our Will*, ==1975==): **rape** keeps all women in fear.

### 3. Core ideas

::: fig cycle | Radical feminism: patriarchy at work in every area of life.
@centre Patriarchy
Family: men as heads, women serve
Sexuality: men's needs as the norm
Body: control of pregnancy and looks
Violence: fear keeps women obedient
Culture: religion, media, literature
:::

#### i) Patriarchy and sexual politics
Male power is not a side effect of capitalism or law; it is a **system of its own**.

#### ii) The personal is political
Housework, marriage and sex are shaped by **power**. Women discovered this through **consciousness-raising** groups, where private complaints — an exhausting double day, a controlling husband — turned out to be shared by all, with a common cause.

#### iii) Bodily autonomy
Women must control their **own bodies** — free from violence, forced marriage and control of reproduction. **Adrienne Rich** (==1976==) separated motherhood as a woman's **experience** from motherhood as an **institution** run by men.

#### iv) Two branches
**Radical-libertarians** (Firestone) wanted to escape rigid gender; **radical-cultural** feminists (Mary Daly) wanted to **celebrate** women's values.

### 4. Achievements and criticisms

#### i) Achievements
Radical feminism made **violence against women** a public issue. It created **rape crisis centres** and **shelters** (the first modern shelter, London, ==1971==), redefined **domestic violence** as a crime, and — through **Catharine MacKinnon** (==1979==) — made **sexual harassment** a form of discrimination.

#### ii) Criticisms
It treated all women as one group, ignoring **race and class** (**bell hooks**). Firestone's focus on biology risks **essentialism**. Separatist writing gave feminism an **anti-men** image, and its language on sexuality provokes **backlash** in conservative societies. Historians add that patriarchy takes **different forms** in different times, so it cannot be one unchanging system.

### 5. Pakistan and conclusion

#### i) Pakistan
The **Women's Action Forum** (==1981==) fought the **Hudood Ordinances** (==1979==), which exposed rape victims to adultery charges. Later laws on **acid attacks** (==2011==), **honour killing** (==2016==) and **rape** (==2021==) reflect the radical insight that violence controls women. The **Aurat March** slogan ***Mera Jism Meri Marzi*** echoed the demand for bodily autonomy — and showed how slogans can provoke controversy.

#### ii) Conclusion
Radical feminism's **analysis** of power and violence has saved lives and changed laws worldwide. Its **style** can alienate potential allies, and its claim that all women share one oppression needs the correction of intersectional thinking. In Pakistan its demands succeed best when argued in the language of the **Constitution** and of Islam's protection of human dignity.

